MODIFIED VERSION, hybrid pre-Wannierization release v3.0 (2026)
****************************************************************

This is a MODIFIED version of wien2wannier, released as version 3.0.
It is based on upstream wien2wannier 2.0 as shipped with WIEN2k, and
it was developed and tested with WIEN2k 24.1. William P. Comaskey
modified and extended it in 2025-2026. This paragraph is the notice
of modification required by section 5 of the GNU GPL.

Changes from wien2wannier 2.0
*****************************

This is the summary.  NEWS lists every change with the files it
concerns.  The case.inwf modes AMN, MMN and BOTH, the def-file units and
the output files of 2.0 are kept.  The numbers w2w writes can differ from
those of 2.0 on the same input, for the reasons given under "Bug fixes"
and "Improvements".

New programs and modes

  * The `wien2wannier' driver (SRC/wien2wannier) runs the whole chain
    from an existing full-mesh WIEN2k case to a hand-off for Wannier90
    and checks its inputs first.  It never runs a DFT calculation.
  * The program `w2w_hybrid' (SRC_w2w/Makefile.hybrid) reads the .amn,
    .mmn, .eig and .nnkp files of w2w and writes a hand-off with
    num_bands = num_wann, which stock Wannier90 localizes with no dis_*
    keywords.
  * case.inwf MODE = AUTO: w2w selects the projections itself from the
    projectability of each atom and l channel.  Band indices 0 and -1 on
    line 2 let it choose the band window as well.
  * MODE = AAMN runs the AUTO selection and writes only the .amn.
  * MODE = HYBR runs the whole hybrid pre-Wannierization inside w2w and
    writes <case>-hyb.{amn,mmn,eig}.
  * The hybrid pre-Wannierization (w2w_hybrid and HYBR) builds per-state
    projectability masks, freezes all states below an energy ceiling
    (froz_emax: default auto in w2w_hybrid and HYBR, 1.0 eV above E_F in
    the driver), minimizes Omega_I under that constraint by Grassmann
    conjugate gradients (default) or Gauss-Seidel sweeps, and builds an
    SCDM-based starting gauge.
  * The window route (`w2w_hybrid --window', `wien2wannier --method
    window') writes the same selection as ordinary Wannier90
    disentanglement windows.
  * New output files: <out>.masks, .anatomy, .dis (outcome of the
    disentanglement and subspace continuity), .anchors, projmap.dat,
    projectability.dat and autoprojection.diag.
  * Scripts in tools/ plot the masks (plot_certificate.py), the pool
    anatomy (plot_anatomy.py) and Wannier bands against the WIEN2k band
    path (band_compare.py).

Improvements to w2w and w2wc

  * MPI: built with -DMPI, w2w and w2wc divide the k-points over ranks
    and rank 0 writes the files.  Without -DMPI they run serially.
  * Faster overlaps and projections (cached A_lm, tabulated Gaunt
    coefficients, zgemm, optional FFTW with -DHAVE_FFTW).  The same
    quantities are computed with the sums in a different order.
  * Spin-orbit in one run: a def file that lists unit 9 makes w2w read
    both spinor components and write the combined .amn and .mmn, instead
    of per-spin runs followed by w2waddsp.  The driver writes such a def
    file; `x w2w -so' does not, and the 2.0 route still works.
  * Each projection line of an AMN or BOTH deck can carry a sixth integer
    that selects the radial function and reaches semicore shells such as
    Ga 3d.
  * W2W_TAILS=1 projects onto full-cell projectors, with each u_l
    continued outside the muffin tin by an exponential tail.
  * Every run tests whether bmin or bmax cuts through a degenerate
    multiplet and reports it.  W2W_EDGES (auto, warn, strict, snap) sets
    the response.  The default, auto, moves only edges chosen by the AUTO
    sentinels and otherwise warns.
  * The .amn carries 16 significant digits instead of 5.  Every .amn and
    .mmn written is accompanied by a full-precision binary copy
    (.amn.bin, .mmn.bin).
  * planew stops with an explanation when a plane-wave index falls
    outside its interstitial grid, and case.outputwf reports more
    diagnostics.

Bug fixes in w2w and w2wc

  * planew dropped part of the interstitial overlap of a (k, k+b) pair
    when k+b had more plane waves than k, and it skipped the last grid
    point.  Both are corrected, which changes some .mmn blocks.
  * With k-parallel vector files (case.def N) the interstitial grid of
    planew came from the last file only.  It covers all files.
  * A bmax larger than the band count in case.energy is clamped with a
    warning.  The eigenvalue buffer of read_vec held at most 1000 bands,
    and vector files with more bands wrote past its end.
  * w2w stops with a message naming the right program when it is given
    complex vector files or w2wc is given real ones.  2.0 finished with
    wrong numbers on complex vectors.
  * Radial functions: Nrf rises from 4 to 5, because an LAPW l with three
    local orbitals needs index 5.  In APW mode a second-derivative local
    orbital (HDLO) was evaluated as a copy of u_l.  It is built as the
    second energy derivative, and the LO coefficients use the computed
    <LO|LO> instead of 1.
  * The first atom of each equivalence class has the identity as its
    local frame.  2.0 used the first symmetry operation that maps the
    atom to itself, so the (l, m) of projections on that atom could refer
    to a rotated frame.  The .amn of such structures changes and the .mmn
    does not.
  * Messages that print an integer or real array no longer stop a
    gfortran-compiled run with "Missing comma between descriptors".
  * A full disk stops the run.  Output files are closed with a size
    check, where 2.0 could leave a truncated file without notice.

Changes to other programs

  * wplot and wplotc build second-derivative local orbitals (HDLO) as the
    second energy derivative of u_l and normalize LO coefficients with
    the computed <LO|LO>.  2.0 plotted such functions wrongly inside the
    muffin tins.  The Nrf fix above (an LAPW l with three local orbitals)
    applies to wplot and wplotc as well.
  * wplot, wplotc and the SRC_trig programs share util_w2w.F with w2w and
    get the same message-format fix.
  * w2waddsp writes the .amn with 16 significant digits, stops on
    up/dn files that differ in dimensions, k-neighbor lists or G-shifts,
    adds .amn entries by (m,n,k) index, copies the whole title line, and
    reads and writes binary copies.  It holds both spin arrays in memory.
  * wplot, wplotc and w2waddsp are built by the top-level `make' but are
    not installed by `make install'.

Build and install

  * `make install' installs w2w, w2wc, w2w_hybrid, w2w_hybrid_acc (when
    built) and the driver as `wien2wannier', and nothing else.  2.0
    built everything and copied every executable file under SRC* to
    $WIENROOT.  `make install-cli' installs the driver alone.
  * The build steps below build only SRC_w2w.  The top-level `make' also
    builds wplot, wplotc and w2waddsp.  w2waddsp links
    SRC_w2w/lib/hybrid_files.o, so SRC_w2w has to be built before
    SRC_trig.
  * `make -f Makefile.hybrid' builds w2w_hybrid and `make -f
    Makefile.hybrid acc' builds the OpenMP program w2w_hybrid_acc.
  * w2w, w2wc, wplot and wplotc identify themselves as v3.0, and the
    package targets WIEN2k 24.1 (WIEN-VERSION), where 2.0 targeted 16.1.

Removals

  * SRC/x_lapw (a copy of the WIEN2k 16.1 `x' script) and
    compile_wien2wannier.sh.  The `x' script of WIEN2k is used.
  * The make targets dist-tmp, wien-tar and wien-dist.
  * No case.inwf mode, def-file unit or output file of 2.0 is removed.

Documentation and tests

  * User guides in doc/hybrid/ (00-public-wien2wannier-cli to
    06-paper-models), docs/reference/mathematical-background.md,
    REFERENCES.md (literature behind each source file), INDEX.md and
    SRC_w2w/INDEX.md (file-by-file maps), CITATION.cff and .zenodo.json.
  * test/test_wien2wannier_cli.py tests the driver with 55 cases.

The symmetry-adapted features of the development branches are not part of
this release.

This modified version remains under the GNU GPL v3 (or later); see COPYING.

Citing this version. Publications that use wien2wannier 3.0 should cite
the original wien2wannier paper, as its authors request (see below),
together with the paper that introduces the hybrid pre-Wannierization and
the archived release:

   J. Kuneš, R. Arita, P. Wissgott, A. Toschi, H. Ikeda and K. Held,
   "Wien2wannier: From linearized augmented plane waves to maximally
   localized Wannier functions", Comput. Phys. Commun. 181, 1888 (2010),
   doi:10.1016/j.cpc.2010.08.005.

   W. P. Comaskey and J. L. Mendoza-Cortes, "Fully Automated Wannier
   Disentanglement via Projectability Selection in Gaussian and Augmented
   Plane-Wave Frameworks" (2026), to be published.

   W. P. Comaskey and J. L. Mendoza-Cortes, wien2wannier 3.0 (2026), Zenodo,
   doi:10.5281/zenodo.23485188.

The calculations of the paper, including the WIEN2k and wien2wannier cases
of its LAPW models, are archived at doi:10.5281/zenodo.23485290.

CITATION.cff carries the same information in machine-readable form.

Building this release (developed and tested with WIEN2k 24.1, see
WIEN-VERSION): copy make.sys.example to make.sys and edit it (macOS ARM:
gfortran + OpenBLAS for w2w/w2wc, e.g. -L/opt/homebrew/opt/openblas/lib
-lopenblas, without Accelerate; Makefile.hybrid links Accelerate,
which its OpenMP build needs on macOS). Then

   cd SRC_w2w && make all              # w2w (real) + w2wc (complex)
   make -f Makefile.hybrid             # w2w_hybrid (serial)
   make -f Makefile.hybrid acc         # w2w_hybrid_acc (OpenMP)
   cd .. && make install target-dir="$WIENROOT"   # optional, see INSTALL

Version 3.0 replaces the programs w2w and w2wc of a WIEN2k installation
and adds w2w_hybrid and the driver; it replaces no script. WIEN2k's own
`x' script writes the def file that w2w 3.0 reads, so this package
contains no copy of it. `make install' copies the programs and the
driver only; `make install-cli target-dir="$HOME/bin"' installs the driver
alone. INSTALL has the details.

Python 3.10 or newer is required for the standard-library-only launcher;
NumPy and SciPy are not required. The normal invocation is:

   wien2wannier --input /path/to/case --seed model --k-grid 8 8 8

This consumes existing DFT vectors and never runs SCF or changes their mesh.
Use `--check` or `--dry-run` to validate without writing or computing.

Public command guide: doc/hybrid/00-public-wien2wannier-cli.md;
method quickstart: doc/hybrid/01-overview-and-quickstart.md;
end-to-end example: doc/hybrid/05-worked-example.md.

Diagnostic figures (numpy + matplotlib): tools/plot_certificate.py draws
the window non-representability certificate from a run's <out>.masks file,
tools/plot_anatomy.py draws the auto-trust anatomy from <out>.anatomy.
Both accept several runs at once, one panel per run. See INDEX.md for the
map of the tree.


Wien2Wannier is an  interface program between Wien2k  and Wannier90 to
obtain maximally localized Wannier functions from Wien2k calculations.
In any published  papers arising from the use  of Wien2Wannier, please
cite:

   J. Kuneš, R. Arita, P. Wissgott, A. Toschi, H. Ikeda, K. Held,
   Comp. Phys. Commun. 181, 1888 (2010)

If you  use additional codes  to process wien2wannier output,  such as
Wannier90 or BerryPI, do not forget  to cite the appropriate papers as
well.

For wien2wannier updates and further information, see
<https://wien2wannier.github.io>

Questions relating to the usage of  wien2wannier should be sent to the
Wien2k mailing list <http://www.wien2k.at/reg_user/mailing_list/>.  As
regards Wannier90 and  the MLWF procedure, there is a  mailing list at
<http://www.wannier.org/forum.html>.

Finally, bug reports, feature requests, etc.  can be submitted at
<https://github.com/wien2wannier/wien2wannier/issues>.  Problems specific to
version 3.0 (the hybrid pre-Wannierization) should be reported at
<https://github.com/wcomaskey/wien2wannier/issues>.


Installation
************

See the file INSTALL.  WIEN2k itself contains wien2wannier 2.0; version
3.0 is built separately and replaces only the programs `w2w' and `w2wc'.

To construct maximally localized Wannier functions, your will need the
Wannier90 program,  available from <http://wannier.org>.  In  order to
use  the two  together, Wannier90  should  be compiled  with the  same
compiler and options as Wien2k.


